; Leg EG -- REGRESSION, send direction.  The Victor SENDS 32,768 bytes BY NAME.
;
; BENCH take-file: there is no "set line" here.  ~/.kermrc names the adapter.
; CHECK THAT IT NAMES THE ONE THAT IS PLUGGED IN.
;
;  START THIS FIRST, THEN THE VICTOR.  The Victor is the initiator on a
;  send leg and gives up if nothing answers its S packets.
;
;  Edit 16's exact range.  Control: SS16ai leg CC / SS16bc leg HB --
;  37,557 wire bytes, 18 packets, 22.207 s, 1,475 cps.
;  The received file lands here; cmp it against trans.dat (d94d2bed...).
;
; READ IT WITH:
;     python3 v9k/tools/pktstat.py --rxbytes <from STEPEG.OUT> s16bdEG.pkt
; Residual -11 clean, +28 with a startup timeout.
; cmp FIRST, then the counters.  A leg that is fast and wrong is the failure.

; THE 27 AUGUST HANG WAS THIS: 'receive <as-name>' makes C-Kermit ask
; "Accept incoming file ...?" on stdin, and stdout is REDIRECTED, so the
; prompt goes into the .host file and the run waits for a key nobody can
; see.  NEXT_SESSION documents this trap for the VICTOR side; it is the
; same defect on the HOST side.  ANY take-file whose stdout is redirected
; must disable every interactive prompt.
set receive confirm off
set exit warning off
set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing cautious
set block 3
set retry 30
log packets s16bdEG.pkt
; EXPLICIT FRESH DESTINATION.  A bare 'receive' takes the name from the
; sender -- FIXTURE.DAT -> fixture.dat -- which already exists here and
; is refused in the A-packet ACK.  That killed this leg on 26 August.
receive goteg.dat
statistics
